<!-- §01 The Problem -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>Knowledge Evaporates When the Context Ends</h2>
<p>
  Suppose you teach a language model something new — say, facts about a novel language — by putting examples in its context window.
  The model adapts beautifully. It answers questions correctly. But the moment you close that context and start fresh, the
  knowledge is gone. The model's parameters were never updated.
</p>
<p>
  Conversely, if you <em>do</em> fine-tune the model on new knowledge, you risk catastrophic forgetting — the weights that encoded
  old knowledge get overwritten by the new. There's no stable middle ground between "ephemeral in-context adaptation" and
  "permanent but destructive fine-tuning."
</p>
<p>
  This is the foundational problem the Sleep paradigm addresses. LLMs have two extreme memory modes:
  the attention mechanism (updates every token, forgets everything at context boundary) and MLP weights (never update after pre-training).
  There is no medium-timescale memory — no place to store "I learned this today, and I want it to persist."
</p>

<!-- The catastrophic forgetting canvas: wake vs sleep -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">wake/sleep lifecycle — knowledge retention over time</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to toggle view</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="lifecycle-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <span class="ctrl-label">Model:</span>
      <button id="btn-icl" class="active-btn">ICL only (no sleep)</button>
      <button id="btn-sleep">Sleep</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The animation above illustrates the core failure mode: without sleep, each new context window acquires temporary knowledge
  that fades completely when the window closes. With sleep, periodic consolidation checkpoints transfer that fragile in-context
  knowledge into stable long-term parameters — knowledge that survives across sessions.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 Memory Spectrum -->
<section id="s-memory" data-code="memory">
<div class="section-no">02 — Memory Spectrum</div>
<h2>The Continuum Memory System: A Spectrum of Frequencies</h2>
<p>
  The key insight behind Hope's architecture is that memory stability is not binary — it's a spectrum.
  Human brains have hippocampal (short-term, high-plasticity) and cortical (long-term, low-plasticity) memory systems
  operating at different timescales. Transformers only have two extreme endpoints.
</p>
<p>
  The <strong>Continuum Memory System (CMS)</strong> generalizes this: a chain of MLP layers, each with its own
  <em>update frequency</em> — how many times it gets updated per "unit of time." Higher-frequency layers are shorter-term
  (update often, forget quickly). Lower-frequency layers are longer-term (update rarely, remember stably).
</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Update Frequency Definition</div>
<span class="math-var">f_W</span> = number of updates per unit of time for weight component <span class="math-var">W</span>
<br>
<span class="math-var">C^(l)</span> = chunk size for l-th layer = max_frequency / <span class="math-var">f_l</span>
<br><br>
Layer chain: MLP<sup>(f1)</sup> → MLP<sup>(f2)</sup> → ... → MLP<sup>(f_k)</sup>
<br>
where <span class="math-var">f1 ≥ f2 ≥ ... ≥ f_k</span>  <span class="math-comment">// faster → slower (more stable)</span>
<br><br>
<span class="math-comment">// Attention: f = ∞ (updates every token, forgets at boundary)</span>
<span class="math-comment">// Pre-trained MLP: f ≈ 0 (never updates at test time)</span>
<span class="math-comment">// CMS fills the gap with intermediate frequencies</span>
</div>

<!-- Frequency spectrum visualization -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">memory frequency spectrum — from ephemeral to permanent</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover over layers</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="spectrum-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The crucial observation: for any layer in this chain, the layers to its left (higher-frequency) hold
  <em>shorter-term, more fragile</em> knowledge. Before those layers get updated (potentially overwriting their knowledge),
  that knowledge must be transferred somewhere more stable. This transfer is exactly what <strong>sleep</strong> accomplishes.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §03 The Sleep Paradigm -->
<section id="s-architecture" data-code="architecture">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Sleep Paradigm</div>
<h2>Two Phases: NREM Consolidation + REM Dreaming</h2>
<p>
  Human sleep has two well-studied phases: slow-wave sleep (NREM) consolidates declarative memories by replaying
  hippocampal patterns to the cortex, and REM sleep actively strengthens newly formed synapses through dreaming.
  The Sleep paradigm maps these directly to two computational phases:
</p>

<div class="step-list" id="sleep-steps">
  <div class="step-box active" id="step-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Wake Phase — In-Context Learning</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Model receives new data and adapts via in-context learning (ICL). High-frequency attention layers update every token. Short-term fragile knowledge accumulates in the fast MLP layers.</div>
      <div class="step-code">attention.update(every_token) + fast_mlp.update(every_chunk)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Sleep Trigger — No New Data</div>
      <div class="step-desc">At scheduled intervals (determined by chunk sizes), the model stops processing external data. The sleep phase begins. All updates from here are self-generated.</div>
      <div class="step-code">if step % C_l == 0: enter_sleep_phase()</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Memory Consolidation (NREM-like)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Upward distillation: knowledge in layer l-1 (faster, more fragile) is transferred to layer l (slower, more stable) via Knowledge Seeding. A new low-rank expert is added to layer l to avoid overwriting existing knowledge.</div>
      <div class="step-code">knowledge_seeding(src=mlp[l-1], dst=mlp[l] + new_expert)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Dreaming (REM-like)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">RL-driven self-improvement: model generates synthetic "dreams" (training data), selects the most informative ones via gradient scoring, fine-tunes on them using LoRA, and uses performance improvement as the reward signal.</div>
      <div class="step-code">dreams = sample_with_random_expert(model, context) → RL reward</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="step-box" id="step-4">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Synaptic Pruning + Resume Wake</div>
      <div class="step-desc">After consolidation, low-rank parameters added to faster layers in previous sleep periods are reset — freeing capacity for future knowledge. The model resumes the wake phase.</div>
      <div class="step-code">mlp[l-1].reset_lora_experts()  # free capacity</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="step-nav">
  <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
  <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
  <span id="step-prog" style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Step 1 of 5</span>
</div>

<p style="margin-top:1.25rem">
  The key architectural choice: each sleep event expands the target layer by adding a <strong>new low-rank expert</strong>
  (parametrized as a LoRA-style A·B matrix). This gives the model capacity to absorb new knowledge without overwriting
  old knowledge — a computational analog of neuroplasticity.
</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 Knowledge Seeding -->
<section id="s-seeding" data-code="seeding">
<div class="section-no">04 — Knowledge Seeding</div>
<h2>Upward Distillation: Smaller-Self Teaches Larger-Self</h2>
<p>
  Standard knowledge distillation flows <em>downward</em>: a large teacher compresses knowledge into a smaller student.
  Knowledge Seeding inverts this: a smaller model (with its recently-updated, fragile short-term knowledge) teaches
  a larger model (the expanded version, with a new low-rank expert added). The student has <em>more capacity</em> than
  the teacher — the goal is not compression but consolidation.
</p>

<!-- Distillation direction SVG -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">knowledge seeding — upward vs traditional distillation</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">contrast: knowledge seeding flows upward</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="seeding-canvas" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The distillation objective has two components. First, an on-policy distillation loss (following GKD) that
  forces the student's output distribution to match the teacher's token-level logits — the student generates data,
  receives token feedback from the teacher. Second, a <strong>Learning to Imitate (LTI)</strong> objective using RL:
  the student is given a prefix from teacher-generated "dreams," must complete the continuation, and is rewarded
  based on both semantic similarity (did it mean the same thing?) and token-level similarity (did it generate nearly
  the same tokens?).
</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Knowledge Seeding Objective</div>
<span class="math-var">L_KS</span> = E_x [ (1-α) · E_y~student [ r(y) ] - α · E_y~student [ D(teacher || student)(y|x) ] ]
<br><br>
<span class="math-comment">// α controls distillation vs imitation balance</span>
<br>
<span class="math-comment">// r(y) = γ·r_sem + (1-γ)·r_abs (semantic + token similarity reward)</span>
<br>
<span class="math-comment">// Only the new low-rank expert is updated — old parameters frozen</span>
</div>

<p>
  Critically: during knowledge seeding, <strong>only the newly added low-rank expert is updated</strong>. All other
  parameters are frozen. This surgical isolation ensures that the transferred knowledge from the fast layer is stored
  in dedicated capacity, never interfering with what the slow layer already knows. After consolidation is complete,
  the fast layer's previously-added low-rank experts are reset — analogous to synaptic pruning.
</p>

