</div>
</section>

<!-- §05 Dreaming -->
<section id="s-dreaming" data-code="dreaming">
<div class="section-no">05 — Dreaming</div>
<h2>RL-Driven Self-Improvement: Learning to Dream Better</h2>
<p>
  After memory consolidation, the model enters the dreaming phase — analogous to REM sleep. Instead of passively
  replaying old data, the model actively generates synthetic training examples ("dreams"), evaluates which dreams
  would most improve its own performance, and fine-tunes on them.
</p>
<p>
  The key challenge: self-improvement in a continual learning setting risks catastrophic forgetting. Each round of
  dreaming must improve the model on new tasks without erasing what it previously learned. The two-stage design of
  Sleep (consolidation first, then dreaming) provides crucial protection: by the time dreaming occurs, the new
  knowledge has already been consolidated into dedicated low-rank experts, isolated from the model's pre-existing knowledge.
</p>

<h3>Three Innovations Over SEAL</h3>
<p>
  Sleep builds on SEAL (Zweiger et al. 2025) but addresses three limitations:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scalability</strong>: SEAL uses supervised fine-tuning in its inner loop, limiting the number of dreams. Sleep replaces SFT with LoRA, enabling far more self-edit iterations.</li>
  <li><strong>Catastrophic Forgetting</strong>: SEAL can erase old knowledge during iterative self-improvement. Sleep's prior consolidation phase provides a stable foundation.</li>
  <li><strong>Exploration</strong>: SEAL samples from the model's current knowledge space only. Sleep adds a <strong>random expert</strong> in each MoE router during dreaming, injecting "irrelevant" knowledge and encouraging the model to synthesize novel combinations of memories.</li>
</ul>

<!-- Dream generation canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">dream generation — gradient-scored selection + random expert exploration</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Play to animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
